FEAT-08: bound the Argon2id parameters of an ODF manifest before deriving the key.

The memory, passes and lanes come straight from the document's manifest. A crafted memory of several GiB is an out-of-memory crash
and a huge pass count is one uninterruptible call (the same class as the PBKDF2 iteration count, which is already bounded). Caps,
checked before anything is derived: memory 1..262144 KiB (256 MiB), passes 1..16, lanes 1..16, memory (KiB) times passes at most
1048576 (a gibibyte-iteration). Stock LibreOffice writes about 64 MiB, 3 passes, 4 lanes. A refusal leaves its reason in
LO_WASM_PACKAGE_REFUSAL (argon2-memory-kib=, argon2-passes=, argon2-lanes=, argon2-cost=) for the bridge to report as
ENCRYPTION_REFUSED.

diff --git a/package/source/zipapi/ZipFile.cxx b/package/source/zipapi/ZipFile.cxx
--- a/package/source/zipapi/ZipFile.cxx
+++ b/package/source/zipapi/ZipFile.cxx
@@ -164,6 +164,14 @@
 constexpr sal_Int32 nMaxPBKDF2IterationCount = 10000000;
 // The longest key any package cipher uses is 32 bytes (AES-256).
 constexpr sal_Int32 nMaxDerivedKeySize = 64;
+// Argon2id parameters come straight from the manifest of the document, so each is bounded before anything is derived
+// (FEAT-08): the memory is allocated up front (a crafted 4 GiB is an out-of-memory crash) and the passes are one
+// uninterruptible call (the same class as the PBKDF2 iteration count above). LibreOffice itself writes about 64 MiB,
+// 3 passes, 4 lanes. Memory is in KiB, as argon2 takes it; the cost is memory (KiB) times passes.
+constexpr sal_Int32 nMaxArgon2MemoryKiB = 256 * 1024;
+constexpr sal_Int32 nMaxArgon2Passes = 16;
+constexpr sal_Int32 nMaxArgon2Lanes = 16;
+constexpr sal_Int64 nMaxArgon2Cost = sal_Int64(1024) * 1024;
 }
 
 uno::Reference< xml::crypto::XCipherContext > ZipFile::StaticGetCipher( const uno::Reference< uno::XComponentContext >& xArgContext, const ::rtl::Reference< EncryptionData >& xEncryptionData, bool bEncrypt )
@@ -195,6 +203,44 @@
     }
     else if (xEncryptionData->m_oArgon2Args)
     {
+        {
+            sal_Int32 const nPasses = ::std::get<0>(*xEncryptionData->m_oArgon2Args);
+            sal_Int32 const nMemoryKiB = ::std::get<1>(*xEncryptionData->m_oArgon2Args);
+            sal_Int32 const nLanes = ::std::get<2>(*xEncryptionData->m_oArgon2Args);
+            // a value below 1 would be converted to a huge unsigned number; libargon2 rejects most of those, but not
